# Exercise: Nested dip
#
# `dip` hides exactly one value while running a quotation. To
# reach further into the stack, nest dips:
#
#   Single dip reaches past 1 value:
#     a b [ quot ] dip      → runs quot on a, preserves b
#
#   Nested dip reaches past 2 values:
#     a b c [ [ quot ] dip ] dip
#                           → runs quot on a, preserves b and c
#
# The outer `dip` hides c; inside its quotation, the inner
# `dip` hides b and runs `quot` on a. When the quotations
# unwind, b and c are restored on top in their original order.
#
# Your task: given stack ( 100 5 3 ), add 50 to the 100 (the
# deepest of the three values), preserving both 5 and 3.
# Result: ( 150 5 3 ).
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# audit:allow(stub-lint-error)
# I AM NOT DONE

: test-dip-deeper ( -- )
    100 5 3
    # Your code here — use nested dip to add 50 to the deepest value
    drop drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Top should be 3
    3 test.assert-eq
    # Second should be 5
    5 test.assert-eq
    # Third should be 150
    150 test.assert-eq
;
